  {
    "id": "rw-s6-am.u9.q.003",
    "lesson": "rw-s6-am.u9.l03",
    "pages": [
      474
    ],
    "source_label": "Application activity 3.15 (Unit 9), question 2a",
    "difficulty": "intermediate",
    "prompt": {
      "text": "The random variable X has the probability distribution below (x = 2, 4, 6 with P(X = x) = a, 2a² − a, a² + a − 1). Find the value of a, exactly or to three decimal places.",
      "tex": "\\begin{array}{c|ccc} x & 2 & 4 & 6 \\\\ \\hline P(X = x) & a & 2a^2 - a & a^2 + a - 1 \\end{array}"
    },
    "answer": {
      "check": "numeric",
      "expected": "2/3",
      "check_args": {
        "tolerance": 0.001
      },
      "accepted": [
        "2/3",
        "0.667",
        "a = 2/3"
      ],
      "display_tex": "a = \\frac{2}{3}",
      "input": "expression"
    },
    "hints": [
      "The probabilities of a distribution add up to 1: a + (2a² − a) + (a² + a − 1) = 1.",
      "That simplifies to 3a² + a − 2 = 0, which factorises as (3a − 2)(a + 1) = 0.",
      "The roots are a = 2/3 and a = −1. A probability cannot be negative: check P(X = 2) = a for each root."
    ],
    "solution": [
      {
        "text": "The probabilities add up to 1.",
        "tex": "a + (2a^2 - a) + (a^2 + a - 1) = 1 \\implies 3a^2 + a - 2 = 0"
      },
      {
        "text": "Factorise.",
        "tex": "(3a - 2)(a + 1) = 0 \\implies a = \\frac{2}{3} \\text{ or } a = -1"
      },
      {
        "text": "a = −1 would make P(X = 2) = −1, which is impossible. With a = 2/3 the probabilities are 2/3, 2/9 and 1/9.",
        "tex": "a = \\frac{2}{3}"
      }
    ],
    "review": {
      "status": "developer-test",
      "reviewed_by": null,
      "date": null
    }
  },
  {
